          ":question/id": "8363d7a8-d552-504e-a21c-ebf886d548c2",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-128198",
          ":question/problem": "Find the general solution to the differential equation ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{x}{y},$ given that $K$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y=\\pm ($ \n{{field-1}}$+K)^{1/2}$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "First, we separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x}{y} \\\\ y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+2C \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+K \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+K} \\\\ y & =\\pm ({x}^{2}+K)^{1/2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=2C$ is an arbitrary constant."
        }
