          {
            ":db/id": 17592186069784,
            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186070004,
              ":tutorial/content": "The differential equation that we just solved was **separable**. A first-order differential equation is separable if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} = h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{g(y)} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = h(x).\n$$\n\nOnce the variables are separated, we can integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{g(y)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{g(y)} & =\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then evaluate both integrals and find the solution. Note that for the solution to be valid, we require $g(y)\\neq 0.$",
              ":tutorial/id": "56fc1c96-3599-5022-8d83-60d4188ccf07",
              ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 6767,
              ":tutorial/title": "First-Order Separable Equations"
            },
            ":step/id": "a58fbcf9-b208-5301-bab1-7af13f7e4794",
            ":step/math-academy-id": 41052,
            ":step/next": {
              ":db/id": 17592186069787
            }
          },
          {
